  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1999-p15.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(\\sec^2 x - \\tan^2 x = 1,\\) we have \\[ \\begin{aligned} &(\\sec x - \\tan x)(\\sec x + \\tan x) \\\\ &\\quad = 1. \\end{aligned} \\] With \\(\\sec x - \\tan x = 2,\\) it follows that \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\tfrac12 = 0.5.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Recall the identity \\(\\sec^2 x - \\tan^2 x = 1\\)",
        "Factor it as \\((\\sec x - \\tan x)(\\sec x + \\tan x)\\)"
      ],
